ALL = []
A = ALL.append

A(('h1', 'What You Will Learn in This Study'))
A(('p', 'By the end of this study, you should be able to:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Trace the words “suffer,” “sufferings,” “tribulation” and “affliction” through Romans 8 and 2 Corinthians 4 and note, for each, who is speaking, who is addressed and what is set beside it.',
    'Read Romans 8:17–25 in order and record what the passage says of heirs, of “this present time,” of the glory to be revealed and of the waiting.',
    'Follow Romans 8:26–39 and mark what each sentence states, including the list of afflictions in verse 35, without carrying the passage beyond its words.',
    'Identify who “we” is in each part of 2 Corinthians 4 and observe how verse 12 sets “us” beside “you.”',
    'Set “light affliction” and “a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory” (2 Corinthians 4:17) beside Romans 8:18 and list where the wording agrees and where it differs.',
    'Test three common readings of the two chapters against their own words, sorting what the text states from what readers add.',
]))
A(('call', 'Study Goal',
   'To read Romans 8:17–39 and 2 Corinthians 4 in the order Paul wrote them, and to set down what each passage says of suffering and of glory, to whom it speaks, and what it leaves unsaid, so that every reading rests on what the chapters state and goes no further.'))
A(('nav', 'Study Outline'))
A(('num', [
    'The Setting: two letters, two addresses, and where the passages stand.',
    'Romans 8:17–25: heirs, the sufferings of this present time and the waiting.',
    'Romans 8:26–30: the sentences between the waiting and the list of afflictions.',
    'Romans 8:31–39: the questions, the list of afflictions and the closing sentences.',
    '2 Corinthians 4:1–15: the ministry, the treasure in earthen vessels and the dying of the Lord Jesus.',
    '2 Corinthians 4:16–5:1: the outward and inward man, light affliction and the eternal weight of glory.',
    'Other Passages and the Two Chapters Side by Side: five passages in their own settings, two tables and three readings tested.',
    'Conclusion, Study Summary, Key Distinctions, Scripture-Tracing, Review, Teaching Outline, Guided Further Study and Final Synthesis.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Introduction'))
A(('p', 'Romans 8 and 2 Corinthians 4 are among the most quoted passages on suffering. A reader meets “the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory” or “our light affliction, which is but for a moment” and the mind moves at once to a doctrine of hardship for every believer in every age. This study slows that movement. It reads the two passages in the order Paul wrote them, asks of each sentence who is speaking and to whom, and records what the text says of suffering and of glory and what it leaves unsaid.'))
A(('p', 'The study does not read the whole of either chapter. Study 46 (Secure in Christ) reads Romans 8:28–39 for the question of security, and Study 52 (Sonship and Adoption), Study 53 (Redemption) and Study 56 (Inheritance) each read parts of Romans 8 for adoption, redemption and heirship. Study 81 (Praying in the Spirit) reads Romans 8:26–27 beside the other passages on praying, and Study 37 (What Does Paul Mean by “New Creation”?) reads 2 Corinthians 5:17. This study asks one narrower question: what do Romans 8:17–39 and 2 Corinthians 4 say about suffering, the glory set beside it, and who “we” is in each?'))
A(('p', 'Two habits govern the work. First, each passage is read in its own place, with its speaker, its audience and the words that bind its sentences. Second, a link between passages is made only where a passage makes it. This reading rests on the passage’s own audience markers and setting. When a text leaves a matter unsaid, the study records that and stops.'))

A(('h1', S1))
A(('p', '1.1 Romans. The letter is addressed “To all that be in Rome, beloved of God, called to be saints” (1:7). Chapter 8 opens with “There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus, who walk not after the flesh, but after the Spirit” (8:1). The passage under study begins at verse 17, in the middle of an argument that has already spoken of the Spirit, of children and of the Spirit’s witness. Verse 17 begins “And if children,” and so continues a sentence of thought already under way.'))
A(('p', '1.2 Second Corinthians. The letter is from “Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy our brother, unto the church of God which is at Corinth, with all the saints which are in all Achaia” (1:1). Chapter 4 follows a chapter about the ministry Paul and his companions have received, and its first word, “Therefore,” ties it to what precedes. The pronoun of the chapter is “we”; the hearers are “you.”'))
A(('call', 'The Question Asked of Every Sentence',
   'Of each sentence the study asks four things: who is speaking, to whom, what is said of suffering or of glory, and what the passage leaves unsaid. The study does not turn a statement about Paul and his companions into a statement about every believer, or the reverse, unless the text does.'))
A(('p', '1.3 The words. The KJV renders a handful of words for hardship in these passages. “Sufferings” stands in Romans 8:18 and in 2 Corinthians 1:5–7, Philippians 3:10 and Colossians 1:24, where the same noun is used. The noun the KJV prints as “tribulation” in Romans 8:35 and 2 Corinthians 1:4 is printed “affliction” in 2 Corinthians 4:17.'))

A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 Verses 17–18. “And if children, then heirs; heirs of God, and joint-heirs with Christ; if so be that we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified together” (8:17). The sentence has two parts joined by a semicolon: the statement that children are heirs, and the clause beginning “if so be that we suffer with him.” The clause is stated as written and the text goes on to its purpose, “that we may be also glorified together.” The next verse gives Paul’s reckoning: “For I reckon that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us” (8:18). The speaker is “I.” The sufferings belong to “this present time,” and the glory is a thing that “shall be revealed.”'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Rom 8:17', '“And if children, then heirs; heirs of God, and joint-heirs with Christ; if so be that we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified together.”', 'Heirs, then a clause: “if so be that we suffer with him.” The purpose is stated: “that we may be also glorified together.”'),
    ('Rom 8:18', '“For I reckon that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us.”', 'The speaker is “I.” “This present time” is set beside a glory that “shall be revealed in us.” The verse compares and states no list of the sufferings.'),
    ('Rom 8:19–21', '“For the earnest expectation of the creature waiteth for the manifestation of the sons of God. For the creature was made subject to vanity, not willingly, but by reason of him who hath subjected the same in hope, Because the creature itself also shall be delivered from the bondage of corruption into the glorious liberty of the children of God.”', 'A new subject, “the creature,” that waits, was “made subject to vanity” and “shall be delivered.” The liberty is “of the children of God.”'),
    ('Rom 8:22–23', '“For we know that the whole creation groaneth and travaileth in pain together until now. And not only they, but ourselves also, which have the firstfruits of the Spirit, even we ourselves groan within ourselves, waiting for the adoption, to wit, the redemption of our body.”', 'The groaning is stated of “the whole creation” and then of “ourselves also.” What is awaited is “the adoption, to wit, the redemption of our body.”'),
    ('Rom 8:24–25', '“For we are saved by hope: but hope that is seen is not hope: for what a man seeth, why doth he yet hope for? But if we hope for that we see not, then do we with patience wait for it.”', 'The close of the passage turns to hope and to waiting “with patience.” The verses state no end to the waiting.'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '2.2 What verse 17 says. The first part of the sentence states a relation (children, heirs, joint-heirs). The second part states a condition-like clause, “if so be that we suffer with him,” and a purpose, “that we may be also glorified together.” The text does not say what the clause does to the heirship stated before it, whether the suffering is the same for every hearer, or what the glory is beyond the word itself. The study does not draw a doctrine of merit, loss or reward from the clause, and it does not set the clause against any other passage; each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '2.3 What verse 18 says. Paul does not say here what the sufferings are. He says they belong to “this present time,” that he reckons them “not worthy to be compared with the glory,” and that the glory “shall be revealed in us.” The verse names no date for the revealing and does not say how the sufferings and the glory are related as cause and effect; it compares them.'))
A(('p', '2.4 Verses 19–23: the creature and “we.” The passage widens in verse 19 to “the creature,” which “waiteth for the manifestation of the sons of God.” The KJV prints “the creature” in verses 19–21 and “the whole creation” in verse 22. The verses state that the creature “was made subject to vanity,” “not willingly,” “by reason of him who hath subjected the same in hope,” and that it “shall be delivered from the bondage of corruption into the glorious liberty of the children of God.” In verse 23 the groaning is stated of “ourselves also,” who “have the firstfruits of the Spirit,” and who wait “for the adoption, to wit, the redemption of our body.” The study records these statements as written. It does not identify the creature beyond the words, give a date for its deliverance or for the manifestation of the sons, or define adoption and redemption; Studies 52 and 53 take those questions up.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Verses 24–25: hope and waiting. “For we are saved by hope” (8:24) opens a short argument that hope is of what is not seen, and verse 25 states the conclusion: “then do we with patience wait for it.” The passage ends on waiting, and the verses do not say how long. Waiting, groaning and “this present time” are the three notes the passage strikes before it turns to the Spirit in verse 26.'))
A(('call', 'What Verse 17 Does and Does Not Say',
   'Verse 17 states that children are heirs and then adds a clause, “if so be that we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified together.” The study reports both parts as written. It does not build from the clause a doctrine of merit, of conditions on the inheritance, or of lost reward, and it does not set the clause against other passages to settle what it means.'))

A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', '3.1 Verses 26–27. “Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmities: for we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit itself maketh intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered” (8:26). The word “groanings” echoes the groaning of verses 22–23. The study reads these verses only as they stand between the waiting and the next verse. Study 81 sets them beside the other passages on praying, and the study does not identify the verses with any other practice.'))
A(('p', '3.2 Verse 28. “And we know that all things work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose” (8:28). The speaker returns to “we know.” The verse names “all things” and does not list them. It names those to whom it speaks of good as “them that love God” and “the called according to his purpose.” The passage does not say whether the sufferings of verse 18 are among “all things,” and the study does not say so either.'))
A(('p', '3.3 Verses 29–30. “For whom he did foreknow, he also did predestinate to be conformed to the image of his Son, that he might be the firstborn among many brethren. Moreover whom he did predestinate, them he also called: and whom he called, them he also justified: and whom he justified, them he also glorified” (8:29–30). The verses state a sequence of five verbs, ending with “glorified.” The study reports the verbs as written. It does not draw a doctrine of the predestination of individuals or of free will from them, and it does not say what moment each verb names. Study 46 reads these verses for security.'))
A(('p', '3.4 What the three verses leave unsaid. Verses 26–30 lie between the waiting of verses 18–25 and the list of afflictions in verse 35. The passage does not say that the “all things” of verse 28 are the sufferings of verse 18, and it does not say that the glory of verse 30 is the glory “revealed in us” in verse 18. The study notes that the same word “glorified” stands in verses 17 and 30 and in no other place in the chapter; the text does not say the two uses name one moment.'))

A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Verses 31–34. The passage turns to questions: “What shall we then say to these things? If God be for us, who can be against us?” (8:31). The questions that follow ask who can be against, who shall lay any thing to the charge “of God’s elect,” and who condemns. The answers are stated in the verses: God “spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all” (8:32); “It is God that justifieth” (8:33); “It is Christ that died, yea rather, that is risen again, who is even at the right hand of God, who also maketh intercession for us” (8:34).'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Rom 8:31–32', '“What shall we then say to these things? If God be for us, who can be against us? He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things?”', 'A question about “these things,” then a question that rests on what God did with “his own Son.”'),
    ('Rom 8:33–34', '“Who shall lay any thing to the charge of God’s elect? It is God that justifieth. Who is he that condemneth? It is Christ that died, yea rather, that is risen again, who is even at the right hand of God, who also maketh intercession for us.”', 'Two questions and two answers. The speaker is not named; the voice is the same “we” of verse 31.'),
    ('Rom 8:35–36', '“Who shall separate us from the love of Christ? shall tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or peril, or sword? As it is written, For thy sake we are killed all the day long; we are accounted as sheep for the slaughter.”', 'A question and a list of seven afflictions. A written sentence is quoted with “As it is written.”'),
    ('Rom 8:37–39', '“Nay, in all these things we are more than conquerors through him that loved us. For I am persuaded, that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, Nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.”', 'The answer: “in all these things.” The speaker returns to “I am persuaded” and lists ten things. The close names “the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.”'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '4.2 The list in verse 35. The question is “Who shall separate us from the love of Christ?” and the list that follows names seven things: “tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or peril, or sword.” The KJV’s “tribulation” here is the ordinary word for affliction, the same noun printed “tribulation” in 2 Corinthians 1:4 and “affliction” in 2 Corinthians 4:17. The study does not link it to any named period elsewhere in Scripture and does not read the list as a forecast; it reads it as a list of the kinds of hardship the verse names. The text does not say that any hearer has met all seven, or any.'))
A(('p', '4.3 The quoted sentence (8:36). “As it is written, For thy sake we are killed all the day long; we are accounted as sheep for the slaughter.” Paul quotes a written sentence and applies it to “we.” The reader who wishes to see its source may trace it in the Psalms; the study records only how Paul introduces it and what he says after it, “Nay, in all these things we are more than conquerors through him that loved us” (8:37).'))
A(('p', '4.4 “In all these things.” Verse 37 says “in all these things,” not “out of” them or “after” them. The verse states that “we are more than conquerors through him that loved us.” The text does not say whether the afflictions end, are lifted or are borne, and it does not say how the conquering takes place beyond the words “through him that loved us.”'))
A(('p', '4.5 The closing list (8:38–39). Verses 38–39 begin “For I am persuaded” and name ten things that shall not be “able to separate us from the love of God.” The list includes “angels,” “principalities,” “powers,” “things present,” “things to come,” “height” and “depth.” The study records the words and gives them no ranking, no identification with persons or places, and no map of the unseen world. The passage speaks of what separates from “the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.” Whether the passage proves a doctrine of security is the question of Study 46, and this study does not take it up.'))
A(('call', 'Seven Afflictions, One Question',
   'Verse 35 asks what shall separate us from the love of Christ and names tribulation, distress, persecution, famine, nakedness, peril and sword. The study reads “tribulation” as the ordinary word for affliction and does not tie it to any named period. The list is Paul’s own question to the saints at Rome, and the study reads it there.'))

A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', '5.1 The ministry. “Therefore seeing we have this ministry, as we have received mercy, we faint not” (4:1). The chapter opens on the ministry and on the mercy received. Verses 2–5 say what the speakers have renounced, what they preach, and whom they serve: “we preach not ourselves, but Christ Jesus the Lord; and ourselves your servants for Jesus’ sake” (4:5). The “we” of these verses is the speakers, set beside “you.” The study reads verses 3–4 (“our gospel,” the “god of this world”) as they stand and does not take them up here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('2 Cor 4:5–6', '“For we preach not ourselves, but Christ Jesus the Lord; and ourselves your servants for Jesus’ sake. For God, who commanded the light to shine out of darkness, hath shined in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.”', 'The “we” preach and are “your servants.” “The glory of God” is named in verse 6, in “the face of Jesus Christ.”'),
    ('2 Cor 4:7', '“But we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency of the power may be of God, and not of us.”', 'The treasure is in “earthen vessels.” The purpose given is that the power “may be of God, and not of us.”'),
    ('2 Cor 4:8–9', '“We are troubled on every side, yet not distressed; we are perplexed, but not in despair; Persecuted, but not forsaken; cast down, but not destroyed.”', 'Four pairs. Each names a hardship and a limit. The “we” is the speakers.'),
    ('2 Cor 4:10–12', '“Always bearing about in the body the dying of the Lord Jesus, that the life also of Jesus might be made manifest in our body. For we which live are alway delivered unto death for Jesus’ sake, that the life also of Jesus might be made manifest in our mortal flesh. So then death worketh in us, but life in you.”', 'The speakers bear “the dying of the Lord Jesus” so that “the life also of Jesus” may be made manifest. Verse 12 sets “us” beside “you.”'),
    ('2 Cor 4:13–15', '“We having the same spirit of faith, according as it is written, I believed, and therefore have I spoken; we also believe, and therefore speak; Knowing that he which raised up the Lord Jesus shall raise up us also by Jesus, and shall present us with you. For all things are for your sakes, that the abundant grace might through the thanksgiving of many redound to the glory of God.”', 'A written sentence is quoted. The knowledge stated is that God “shall raise up us also by Jesus, and shall present us with you.”'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '5.2 The vessels (4:7). The treasure is “in earthen vessels,” and the reason is stated: “that the excellency of the power may be of God, and not of us.” The verse says what the vessels are for and does not say who the vessels are beyond “we.”'))
A(('p', '5.3 The four pairs (4:8–9). Each pair names a hardship (“troubled,” “perplexed,” “persecuted,” “cast down”) and a limit (“not distressed,” “not in despair,” “not forsaken,” “not destroyed”). The verses state both halves of each pair. They do not say that every hardship has a limit stated in the same way elsewhere, and they do not say what each limit consisted of.'))
A(('p', '5.4 “The dying of the Lord Jesus” (4:10–12). The speakers are “always bearing about in the body the dying of the Lord Jesus,” and are “alway delivered unto death for Jesus’ sake,” so that “the life also of Jesus might be made manifest.” The verses join two statements: the dying is borne, and the life is made manifest. Verse 12 then draws a line between “us” and “you”: “So then death worketh in us, but life in you.” The study records the line and does not say whether “you” are also said to bear the dying; the text does not say so in these verses.'))
A(('p', '5.5 Verses 13–15. The speakers quote a written sentence, “I believed, and therefore have I spoken,” and say “we also believe, and therefore speak.” They state what they know: that God “shall raise up us also by Jesus, and shall present us with you.” The verbs are future, and “us” and “you” are named together. Verse 15 gives the end: “that the abundant grace might through the thanksgiving of many redound to the glory of God.” The passage does not give a date for the raising.'))

A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', '6.1 The outward and inward man. “For which cause we faint not; but though our outward man perish, yet the inward man is renewed day by day” (4:16). The chapter returns to “we faint not,” the phrase of verse 1, and sets “outward man” beside “inward man.” The words “perish” and “is renewed day by day” are stated as written; the verse does not define either man.'))
A(('p', '6.2 “Light affliction” (4:17). The sentence has a subject (“our light affliction”), two qualifiers (“which is but for a moment”) and a verb (“worketh for us”), and its object is “a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory.” The word “affliction” is the same noun the KJV prints “tribulation” in Romans 8:35 and 2 Corinthians 1:4. The verse does not say whether the affliction earns the glory, produces it or accompanies it; the verb is “worketh,” and the text goes no further than the word. The study does not set the sentence against Romans 8:17 as a rule of merit, and it does not say that Romans 8:17 and this sentence state one doctrine.'))
A(('p', '6.3 What is looked at (4:18). The verse says “we look not at the things which are seen, but at the things which are not seen.” The reason is stated: “the things which are seen are temporal; but the things which are not seen are eternal.” The verse does not list either set of things, and it does not say how the looking is done.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Where the study stops (5:1). Second Corinthians 5:1 begins “For we know that if our earthly house of this tabernacle were dissolved, we have a building of God, an house not made with hands, eternal in the heavens.” The verse is read here as the closing sentence of the thought that begins in 4:16. The verses that follow (5:2–10) and the sentence of 5:17 are read in other studies, and this study does not add to them.'))
A(('call', 'Who Is “We” in Second Corinthians 4?',
   'In 4:1–5 the speakers are those who have “this ministry,” and in 4:12 “us” is set against “you.” In 4:13–18 the study keeps the same question in front of each verse and extends a statement beyond Paul and his companions only where the text does so. It does not use the chapter as a list of promises to every reader.'))

A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 Five passages in their own settings. Other letters of Paul speak of suffering in their own places. Each is read here for its speaker, its hearers and its words, and no passage is made to answer another. Philippians and 2 Timothy take the letter-form line: this reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ('Phil 1:29–30', '“For unto you it is given in the behalf of Christ, not only to believe on him, but also to suffer for his sake; Having the same conflict which ye saw in me, and now hear to be in me.”', 'Paul says to the Philippians that suffering “is given.” “Ye saw in me” and “now hear to be in me” name Paul’s own conflict.'),
    ('Phil 3:10–11', '“That I may know him, and the power of his resurrection, and the fellowship of his sufferings, being made conformable unto his death; If by any means I might attain unto the resurrection of the dead.”', 'Paul’s own aim, stated with “I.” The verses do not say that every hearer shares the aim.'),
    ('Col 1:24', '“Who now rejoice in my sufferings for you, and fill up that which is behind of the afflictions of Christ in my flesh for his body’s sake, which is the church.”', 'The sufferings are Paul’s, “for you” and “for his body’s sake.” The phrase “that which is behind of the afflictions of Christ” is reported as written and is not explained here.'),
    ('2 Cor 1:5–7', '“For as the sufferings of Christ abound in us, so our consolation also aboundeth by Christ. And whether we be afflicted, it is for your consolation and salvation, which is effectual in the enduring of the same sufferings which we also suffer: or whether we be comforted, it is for your consolation and salvation. And our hope of you is stedfast, knowing, that as ye are partakers of the sufferings, so shall ye be also of the consolation.”', 'The same letter as chapter 4. “Us” and “you” are set side by side, and “ye are partakers of the sufferings” is stated of the hearers.'),
    ('2 Tim 2:11–12', '“It is a faithful saying: For if we be dead with him, we shall also live with him: If we suffer, we shall also reign with him: if we deny him, he also will deny us.”', 'A “faithful saying” in four clauses. The KJV’s “suffer” in verse 12 is the word it prints “endure” in verse 10. The verses name no time for the reigning.'),
], [0.15, 0.48, 0.37]))
A(('p', '7.2 Other audiences. First Peter, Hebrews 12 and Matthew 5:10–12 also speak of suffering. Each is addressed to its own hearers and is read in its own place in other studies; this study does not set them beside Romans 8 or 2 Corinthians 4.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The two chapters side by side. The first table sets the wording of the two passages in the order of the questions the study asks of each.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Question', 'Romans 8:17–39', '2 Corinthians 4'],
    ['Who speaks', '“I reckon” (8:18); “I am persuaded” (8:38); “we know” (8:22, 28)', '“we” of the ministry (4:1, 5); “we” and “you” set apart (4:12, 14)'],
    ['The hardship named', '“the sufferings of this present time” (8:18); seven afflictions (8:35)', '“light affliction” (4:17); four pairs (4:8–9); “the dying of the Lord Jesus” (4:10)'],
    ['The time named', '“this present time” (8:18); “until now” (8:22)', '“but for a moment” (4:17); “day by day” (4:16)'],
    ['The glory named', '“the glory which shall be revealed in us” (8:18); “glorified” (8:17, 30)', '“a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory” (4:17); “the glory of God” (4:6, 15)'],
    ['The waiting', '“waiting for the adoption” (8:23); “with patience wait” (8:25)', '“shall raise up us also” (4:14); “we look not at the things which are seen” (4:18)'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '7.4 What the two chapters leave unsaid. The second table lists matters neither passage states.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Matter', 'What the passages state', 'What they leave unsaid'],
    ['The cause of the glory', '“if so be that we suffer” (Rom 8:17); “worketh for us” (2 Cor 4:17)', 'Whether the suffering earns, produces or accompanies the glory.'],
    ['Who bears the dying', '“Always bearing about in the body the dying of the Lord Jesus” (2 Cor 4:10)', 'Whether every hearer is said to bear it; 4:12 sets “us” beside “you.”'],
    ['The date of the glory', '“shall be revealed in us” (Rom 8:18); “shall raise up us” (2 Cor 4:14)', 'A date, an event or a link to any other passage on time.'],
    ['The creature', '“the creature … shall be delivered” (Rom 8:21)', 'Which creatures, and when.'],
    ['“All things”', '“all things work together for good” (Rom 8:28)', 'Whether the sufferings of 8:18 are included.'],
    ['The afflictions listed', '“tribulation, or distress, or persecution” (Rom 8:35)', 'Whether any hearer has met them, and any link to a period named elsewhere.'],
], [0.24, 0.38, 0.38]))

A(('h1', 'Conclusion'))
A(('p', 'Romans 8:17–39 and 2 Corinthians 4 each speak of hardship and of glory, and each does so in its own voice. Romans 8 reckons the sufferings of this present time against a glory to be revealed, sets the groaning of the creature and of “ourselves also” side by side, and asks, with a list of seven afflictions, who shall separate from the love of Christ. Second Corinthians 4 speaks for those who hold a treasure in earthen vessels, bear about the dying of the Lord Jesus, call their affliction light and look at the things not seen. The two passages share words and do not state one doctrine. The study has recorded what each passage states, to whom, and where it stops.'))

A(('h1', 'Study Summary'))
A(('bul', [
    'Romans 8:17–18 states heirs, a clause beginning “if so be that we suffer with him,” and Paul’s reckoning that “the sufferings of this present time” are “not worthy to be compared with the glory which shall be revealed in us.”',
    'Romans 8:19–25 widens to “the creature,” sets its groaning beside that of “ourselves also,” and ends on hope and patient waiting.',
    'Romans 8:26–39 states the Spirit’s help, “all things,” a sequence of verbs ending in “glorified,” and a list of seven afflictions answered by “more than conquerors.”',
    '2 Corinthians 4:1–15 speaks for the ministry: a treasure in earthen vessels, four pairs of hardship and limit, and “death worketh in us, but life in you.”',
    '2 Corinthians 4:16–5:1 sets the outward and inward man and “light affliction” beside “a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory,” and the study stops at 5:1.',
]))

A(('h1', 'Key Distinctions to Retain'))
A(('bul', [
    'Who speaks matters: “I” in Romans 8:18 and 8:38, “we” of the ministry in 2 Corinthians 4, and “us” set beside “you” in 4:12. The study does not turn one into the other.',
    'The word for hardship varies: “sufferings,” “tribulation,” “affliction” are named as written, and “tribulation” in Romans 8:35 is not linked to any period named elsewhere.',
]))

